    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0079",
      "sequence": 91,
      "meaning": "The sages criticize Nārada to test her: his instruction made Dakṣa’s sons abandon home, ruined Citraketu’s household and brought the same condition upon Hiraṇyakaśipu. Whoever hears his teaching becomes a homeless mendicant. Deceitful within though outwardly saintly, he wants everyone made like himself. Trusting him, she seeks a naturally detached husband—qualityless, shameless, ill-clad, skull-bearing, familyless, homeless, naked and snake-wearing. What happiness could such a groom give? Satī once married Śiva on the Five’s advice but was later slighted and died. Śiva now sleeps carefree and begs his food; how could a woman ever suit the home of one innately solitary?",
